## Introduction to R Markdown Notebooks

In the last lesson you saw what is a R script, now you are going to learn to use **R notebooks (.Rmd)**.

This type of file **combines both code and text in a single file**, so you can have a full detailed document with your code and explanations ordered.

In the top of document source code you can see a configuration block called `yaml`, in this block we defined the document name (`title`) and the kind of output we want when rendering the document (`output`), by default it will be an html file. See how the output is generated pressing `Knit` button.

You can write text using Markdown syntax and write code using the code chunks like the one below. You will see that the output of the code is displayed below.

```{r}
# This is a code chunk
print("Hello World")
```

During the course we will use both scripts and notebooks to understand the different concepts.

## Variables

As we have seen you can use variables in R to store values and intermediate results and use them later. For assigning variables we use `<-` notation.

```{r}
# We define variable 'a' that stores number 10
a <- 10
a
```

```{r}
# We increase by 3 and store the result on new variable 'b'
b <- a + 3
b
```

You can control all variables in your environment tab of RStudio.

> **Tip:** When naming a variable you can't use special characters like '\$','\@', etc.

```{r}
# Assign to 'a' a new value, what happens to the old one?
```

## Introduction to Functions

In R we have some predefined functions. Functions are small programs designed to support quick operations and be reusable each time we need it.

For example function `mean`:

```{r}
mean(1:10)
```

We have a lot of different useful functions like `min`, `max`, `log`, etc. If you have any doubt on how to use them type `? function_name` to see the function documentation.

## Basic Types

For programming R uses different data types that we need to know and understand:

### numeric

```{r}
c <- 5
d <- 6
c + d
```

Use function `class` to know the object you are working with...

```{r}
class(d)
```

Inside `numeric` class we can have different sub-types...

```{r}
# typeof checks the inner type inside the class
typeof(d)
typeof(1.5)
typeof(1L) # integer
```

### character

```{r}
sentence <- "may the force be with you"
number <- "24"

class(sentence)
```

You can convert objects from one type to another...

```{r}
10 + as.numeric(number)
```

